            <ArticleTitle>MicroRNA-29b promotes the adipogenic differentiation of human adipose tissue-derived stromal cells.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1097-105</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/oby.21467</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE">Obesity is primarily characterized by the accumulation of large amounts of fat in adipose tissue. Within the adipose tissue, adipocytes are derived from adipose tissue-derived stromal cells (ADSCs) via a specialized cell lineage differentiation process, and ADSCs play a key role in the generation and metabolism of adipose tissue. This study investigated whether microRNAs (miRNAs) play a role in adipocyte differentiation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS">Using luciferase reporter and ChIP assays, the relationship between miR-29b, SP1, and TNF-α was examined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">During the normal adipogenic differentiation of ADSCs, up-regulation of miR-29b promoted adipogenesis by enhancing SP1-mediated inhibition of TNF-α.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS">This study investigated the regulatory role of miR-29b during the adipogenic differentiation of ADSCs and found that miR-29b is an effective positive regulator of adipogenesis.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 The Obesity Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
